        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">2</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain how <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Fence wire with sharp points twisted into it, used to keep cattle in or out.">barbed wire</a> and the windmill closed the open range, and how ranching became a business run by <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A business owned by many investors, who share its profits and risks.">corporation</a>s.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">3</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain how the work of a cowboy changed, and why Texas began to <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="To control something by rules and laws.">regulate</a> ranching and land.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; THE BARONS</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Cattle Barons</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The cattle drives made a few ranchers very rich. People called them cattle barons. Several of them began before the Civil War, and they grew with the markets that came after it.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Richard King started the King Ranch in South Texas in 1853. He was a former steamboat captain. He built it into one of the largest ranches in the world, and it still covers more than 800,000 acres. Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove cattle west and north in 1866 along what became the Goodnight-Loving Trail, to feed soldiers and Native people on reservations in New Mexico, and to find new markets in Colorado. Loving was wounded in a fight with Comanche raiders on the way, and he died. Goodnight carried his friend&rsquo;s body hundreds of miles home to Weatherford for burial, as he had promised.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">John Chisum, who grew up in North Texas, built a herd of tens of thousands along the Pecos River in New Mexico. He is a different man from Jesse Chisholm, for whom the Chisholm Trail was named. W. T. Waggoner built a ranch in North Texas that grew to over half a million acres. In 1911, workers on the Waggoner Ranch drilled for water and found oil instead. C. C. Slaughter ran cattle on the South Plains and became a Dallas banker. These ranchers took risks and worked hard. They also had the good fortune of cheap land and a growing nation that wanted beef.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; THE WIRE</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Wire and the Windmill</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1874, an Illinois farmer named Joseph Glidden patented a new kind of fence: wire twisted with sharp barbs. It was cheap, strong, and easy to build. A story says that in 1876, a salesman fenced a pen of wild Texas longhorns in San Antonio&rsquo;s Military Plaza to show that the wire could hold them. Whether or not it happened just that way, ranchers soon saw that <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Fence wire with sharp points twisted into it, used to keep cattle in or out.">barbed wire</a> would let them keep cattle on their own land, and keep other herds off it.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Open-range ranchers hated it. Fences cut off trails, and cut off water holes that had been free to everyone. In 1883, in a drought year, angry cowboys and small ranchers began to cut fences across Texas. The trouble spread to dozens of counties. People were hurt and killed. In 1884, the legislature made fence cutting a felony, and it also required fence builders to put in gates. The open range was ending.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Texas historian Walter Prescott Webb, who was born in East Texas in 1888 and taught at the University of Texas, later wrote that three tools made it possible to settle the dry Great Plains: the Colt revolver, barbed wire, and the windmill. Each did a different job. The revolver helped defend against attack, the wire marked ownership, and the windmill gave water. Webb&rsquo;s idea is a good reminder that machines can change a landscape, though no one tool does it alone.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Fences and wells changed the land itself. Windmills let cattle graze where there had been no water, and herds grew. Many ranchers put more cattle on the range than it could feed. This <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Letting too many animals eat the grass until it cannot grow back.">overgrazing</a> tore up the grass, and mesquite brush spread across ground where grass had been thick. A mid-1880s drought and a fall in cattle prices ruined many ranchers who had borrowed to buy too many animals.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; WHICH TOOL?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Walter Prescott Webb named three tools that made it possible to settle the dry Great Plains. For each row, decide which tool it describes.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Wire let a rancher keep cattle in and other herds out." data-hint="Think about which tool makes a fence.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. It marked who owned the land.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The Colt revolver</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Barbed wire</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The windmill</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. Wire let a rancher keep cattle in and other herds out.">Right. Wire let a rancher keep cattle in and other herds out.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. Windmills pumped water up from underground." data-hint="Think about which tool lifts water out of the ground.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. It gave cattle water far from any river.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The Colt revolver</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Barbed wire</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The windmill</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Think about which tool lifts water out of the ground.">Think about which tool lifts water out of the ground.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. The revolver let a rider fire again and again from horseback." data-hint="Think about the weapon the Rangers carried at Walker’s Creek.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. It helped settlers defend themselves against attack.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The Colt revolver</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Barbed wire</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The windmill</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. The revolver let a rider fire again and again from horseback.">Right. The revolver let a rider fire again and again from horseback.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Fence cutting spread to dozens of counties, and in 1884 the legislature made it a felony." data-hint="Think about which tool cut off trails and water holes that had been free to everyone.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">4. Angry cowboys and small ranchers cut it in the drought year of 1883.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The Colt revolver</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Barbed wire</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The windmill</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. Fence cutting spread to dozens of counties, and in 1884 the legislature made it a felony.">Right. Fence cutting spread to dozens of counties, and in 1884 the legislature made it a felony.</div>
  </div>
