</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; A NEW BUSINESS</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Ranching Becomes a Business</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Ranching now needed more money than most families had. Land, fences, windmills, and a large herd all cost a lot. So <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A business owned by many investors, who share its profits and risks.">corporation</a>s moved in. Investors in Chicago, Scotland, and England put their money into Texas ranches. In 1879, the Matador Ranch began in the Panhandle, and in 1882 it was sold to a Scottish company. The biggest of all was the XIT. The Texas government had no money to pay for a new state capitol, so it traded three million acres of Panhandle land to a Chicago group in exchange for building one. The group turned the land into a ranch that stretched across ten counties.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The cowboy changed too. In the days of the drives, many cowboys hoped to start herds of their own. By the 1880s, ranches were big companies, and a cowboy worked for a <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Money paid to a worker for work done, usually by the day, week, or month.">wage</a>, usually about $25 to $40 a month. In 1883, dozens of cowboys in the Panhandle went on strike for higher pay. The ranch owners refused, and the strike failed. The cowboy&rsquo;s life was still hard, but now he worked for someone else.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Government Steps In</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Texans began to ask whether companies from far away should own so much of the state. In 1891, Governor James Stephen Hogg signed a law limiting how much land foreign owners could hold. The same year, Texas created the Railroad Commission to <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="To control something by rules and laws.">regulate</a> what railroads could charge, since ranchers and farmers depended on them. It was the beginning of a regulatory era in Texas.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Meanwhile, settlers kept coming. Railroads sold land to farmers, and new towns grew up. Farmers plowed the grass where ranches had been and planted wheat and cotton. For a time, the rains came and the crops grew. But the grass had held the soil in place for thousands of years. When it was gone and the rains stopped, the wind would take the soil away. Texans of a later generation would pay for that.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-reflect" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; QUICK CHECK-IN</div>
  <div class="widget-question">A new tool helps you but hurts your neighbor, the way barbed wire hurt small ranchers. What would you want your community to do? (No wrong answer. Just check in with yourself.)</div>
  <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-response="Texas did something like this. In 1884 the legislature made fence cutting a felony and required fence builders to put in gates. Rules can protect both the owner and the neighbor.">&#9878; Make rules so the tool is used fairly</button><button type="button" data-response="Sometimes that works. But when people have no fair way to settle a dispute, quarrels can turn violent. The fence cutting of 1883 hurt and killed people in dozens of counties.">&#128170; Let people work it out themselves</button><button type="button" data-response="Ranching now cost more than most families had. Land, fences, windmills and a large herd were out of reach for small ranchers, and corporations moved in. Helping people adjust is one way a community holds together.">&#129309; Help those who cannot afford the new way</button></div>
  <div class="widget-feedback" data-ph="Ranching now cost more than most families had. Land, fences, windmills and a large herd were out of reach for small ranchers, and corporations moved in. Helping people adjust is one way a community holds together.">Ranching now cost more than most families had. Land, fences, windmills and a large herd were out of reach for small ranchers, and corporations moved in. Helping people adjust is one way a community holds together.</div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">The cowboy of the open range is the one people remember, but the ranch of wire, windmills, and wages was the one that lasted. When a new tool arrives, ask who gains, who loses, and what it does to the land.</p>
